
------WHAT IS REST API??---------


A REST API (Representational State Transfer Application Programming Interface) is a way for different software systems to communicate with each 
other over the internet using standard HTTP methods. It's essentially a set of rules and guidelines for building web APIs that allow clients and 
servers to interact, typically by exchanging data in formats like JSON. 

Key Concepts:

API (Application Programming Interface):
An API is a set of rules and specifications that allow different software applications to communicate and interact with each other. 

REST (Representational State Transfer):
REST is an architectural style for designing networked applications. It defines a set of constraints that, when followed, lead to more flexible, scalable, and maintainable web services. 

HTTP Methods:
REST APIs use standard HTTP methods like GET (retrieve data), POST (create data), PUT (update data), and DELETE (remove data) to perform operations on resources. 

Resources:
In a REST API, resources are any data or content that can be accessed or manipulated by the API, such as users, products, or orders. 

JSON (JavaScript Object Notation):
JSON is a lightweight data-interchange format that is commonly used in REST APIs for exchanging data between client and server. 

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
✅ 1. Monolith (Monolithic Architecture):
A monolith is a single, unified software application where all functions—UI, business logic, database—are part of one codebase and are deployed together.

🧱 Example:
E-commerce app where user authentication, product management, and payment are all tightly integrated into a single app.

✅ Pros:
-->Easy to develop initially
-->Simpler deployment

❌ Cons:
-->Harder to scale specific features
-->Difficult to update or maintain as it grows
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
✅ 2. Microservices Architecture:
In microservices, the application is divided into small, independent services that communicate over APIs.

🧩 Example:
User service, product service, and payment service are separate, and can be developed, deployed, and scaled independently.

✅ Pros:
Easier to scale and maintain
Technology-agnostic (each service can use a different language or framework)

❌ Cons:
Complex communication between services
Requires DevOps and monitoring setup
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
✅ 3. Scaling (Horizontal vs Vertical):
🔹 Vertical Scaling (Scale-Up):
Increasing the power of a single machine (CPU, RAM, etc.).

🖥️ Example: Upgrade your server from 8GB RAM to 32GB.

✅ Easy to implement
❌ Limited by hardware

🔹 Horizontal Scaling (Scale-Out):
Adding more machines or servers to handle load.

🌐 Example: Run your app on 5 servers instead of 1.

✅ Better for large-scale apps
❌ More complex infrastructure
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
✅ 4. API (Application Programming Interface):
An API allows different software applications to communicate with each other.

📬 Example:
A weather app using an API to fetch temperature data from a remote server.

🛠️ Common Types:
REST API
GraphQL
SOAP
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
✅ 5. API Gateway:
An API Gateway is a server that acts as a single entry point into a microservices system.
-->It is a connection between computers or between computer programs.
-->It allows different software systems to communicate and interact with each other. It acts as a messenger ,enabling one application to request services or data from another without 
needing to know the internal workings of the other system.

🚪 Responsibilities:
Route requests to appropriate services
Handle authentication, rate limiting, logging, etc.

🧭 Example: Amazon API Gateway, Kong, NGINX
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
✅ 6. CDN (Content Delivery Network):
A CDN is a network of servers distributed globally to deliver static content like images, CSS, JS faster to users based on their location.

📦 Example:
Cloudflare, Akamai, AWS CloudFront

✅ Improves website speed and performance
✅ Reduces load on your main server